 "blocks": [
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_b5KzyjnFxRygqtmk",
   "name": "Geometric Sequences",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Geometric Sequences"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a geometric sequence?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- In a **geometric sequence**, there is a **common ratio **between consecutive terms in the sequence\n\n  - This means each term is **multiplied** by a common ratio to get the **next term**\n- The **first term** of the sequence is denoted by $a$\n- The common ratio is denoted by $r$\n\n  - For example, 2, 6, 18, 54, 162, … is a sequence with the rule *‘start at two and multiply each number by three’*\n  \n    - The **first term, **$a$***,***** **is 2\n    - The **common ratio, **$r$**, **is 3\n- A geometric sequence can be\n\n  - **increasing **(*r* > 1), or\n  - **decreasing **(0 < *r *< 1)\n- If the common ratio is a **negative number** the terms will alternate between positive and negative values\n\n  - For example, 1, -4, 16, -64, 256, … is a sequence with the rule *‘start at one and multiply each number by negative four’*\n  \n    - The **first term, **$a$***,***** **is 1\n    - The **common ratio, **$r$**, **is -4\n- **Terms** in a geometric sequence can be referred to\n\n  - by the letter $u$* *with\n  - a subscript corresponding to its place in the sequence\n  \n    - e.g.  $u_{1}=a$ is the first term, $u_{9}$ is the ninth term, $u_{n}$ is the $n$<sup>th</sup> term, etc."
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_c8RQZfhSvPzK9dGS",
   "name": "Geometric Series",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Geometric Series"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is a geometric series?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- When the terms of a **geometric sequence** are added together, that is known as a **geometric series**\n\n  - The **terms** (1st term, 2nd term, 3rd term, etc.) are **exactly the same** in the sequence and series\n  - But with the series we're most interested in what happens when the terms are **added together**"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find a term in a geometric series?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The $n^{th}$<sup>*** ***</sup>**term formula** for a geometric series is"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$u_{n}=ar^{n-1}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Where $a$* *is the **first term**, and $r$ is the **common ratio**\n- This is **not **given on the exam **formula sheet**, so make sure you know it\n- The formula allows you to find **any term** in the geometric series\n\n  - **Enter** the values of $a$, $r$ and $n$ and calculate the value of $u_{n}$\n- Sometimes you will be **given a term** and asked to f**ind **$a$** or **$r$\n\n  - **Substitute** the information you have into the formula and solve the equation\n- Sometimes you will be **given two or more consecutive terms** and asked to **find both **$a$** and **$r$\n\n  - Find the **common ratio** by **dividing** a term by the one immediately before it\n  \n    - $r=\\frac{u_{n+1}}{u_{n}}$\n  - **Substitute** this and one of the terms into the formula to find the **first term**\n- Sometimes you may be **given a term along with **$a$** and **$r$ and asked to **find the value of **$n$\n\n  - You can solve this using **logarithms** on your calculator"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I find the sum of a geometric series?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- A **geometric series** is the sum of the terms in a **geometric sequence**\n\n  - For the geometric sequence 2, 6, 18, 54, … the geometric series is 2 + 6 + 18 + 54 + …\n- Use the following **formula** to find the sum of the first *n* terms of a geometric series:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$S_{n}=\\frac{a(1-r^{n})}{1-r}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- $a$<sub>* *</sub>is the **first term**\n- $r$ is the **common ratio**\n- The formula is given on the exam **formula sheet**\n\n  - So you don't need to remember it\n  - But you do need to know how to use it!\n- If $r>1$ the following **rearrangement** of the formula might be more convenient:"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "$S_{n}=\\frac{a(r^{n}-1)}{r-1}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This version is **not** on the formula sheet\n- The formula sheet version will always work as well\n- A question will often **give you the sum of a certain number of terms** and ask you to **find the values of **$a$**, **$r$** or **$n$\n\n  - **Substitute** the information you have into the formula and solve the equation"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- The formula for the **sum** of a geometric series is on the exam formula sheet\n\n  - But the *n*<sup>th</sup> term formula is **not** on the formula sheet\n- You will sometimes need to use logarithms to answer geometric series questions\n\n  - Make sure you are confident doing this\n  - And know how your calculator handles logarithms"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "The sixth term of a geometric series is 486 and the seventh term is 1458.\n\nFind\n\n(a) the common ratio of the series\n\n> *Find the common ratio by dividing a term by the term immediately before it*\n*Here *$r=\\frac{7^{\\mathrm{th}}\\mathrm{term}}{6^{\\mathrm{th}}\\mathrm{term}}$\n\n$r=\\frac{1458}{486}$\n\n$r=3$\n\n(b) the first term of the series.\n\n> *Use the **n*<sup>*th*</sup>* term formula  *$u_{n}=ar^{n-1}$\n\n> *For the 6*<sup>*th*</sup>* term, we know  *$u_{6}=486$*,  *$r=3$*  and  *$n=6$\n\n`table row 486 equals cell a cross times 3 to the power of open parentheses 6 minus 1 close parentheses end exponent end cell row 486 equals cell a cross times 3 to the power of 5 end cell row 486 equals cell 243 a end cell row a equals cell 486 over 243 end cell end table`\n\n$a=2$"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "The first term of a geometric series is 25, and the common ratio is 0.8.\n\nFind the value of the fifth term, as well as the sum of the first 5 terms.\n\n> *To find the fifth term, use the **n*<sup>*th*</sup>* term formula  *$u_{n}=ar^{n-1}$\n*Here  *$n=5$*,  *$a=25$*  and  *$r=0.8$* *\n\n`table row cell fifth space term end cell equals cell 25 cross times 0.8 to the power of open parentheses 5 minus 1 close parentheses end exponent end cell row blank equals cell 25 cross times 0.8 to the power of 4 end cell end table`\n\n> $\\mathrm{fifth} \\mathrm{term}=10.24$\n\n*To find the sum of the first 5 terms, use the sum of a geometric series formula  *$S_{n}=\\frac{a(1-r^{n})}{1-r}$\n*Again,  *$a=25$*  and  *$r=0.8$* *\n\n`S subscript 5 equals fraction numerator 25 open parentheses 1 minus 0.8 to the power of 5 close parentheses over denominator 1 minus 0.8 end fraction`\n\n> *Use your calculator to work this out  *\n\n$S_{5}=84.04$"
  },
  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_DqNff4JYrm6vQjyS",
   "name": "Sum to Infinity",
   "definition": null
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Sum to Infinity"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "What is the sum to infinity of a geometric series?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- The **sum to infinity** is the sum of **all the terms** in a geometric series\n\n  - $u_{1}+u_{2}+u_{3}+...$  'all the way to infinity'\n- **As **$n$** increases** the terms of a geometric series may\n\n  - move **further away** from zero\n  \n    - if  $r>1$  or  $r<-1$\n  - stay **the same distance away** from zero\n  \n    - if  $r=1$  or  $r=-1$\n  - get **closer and closer** to zero\n  \n    - if  $-1<r<1$\n- If the terms are **getting closer to zero** then the series is said to **converge**"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- This means that the **sum of the series** will approach a finite 'limiting value'\n- As $n$ increases, the sum of the terms will get **closer and closer** to the limiting value\n- The limiting value is the **sum to infinity** of the series\n\n  - It is denoted by $S_{\\infty}$"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 3,
   "text": "How do I calculate the sum to infinity?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- First you need to consider the **value of **$r$\n\n  - If  `open vertical bar r close vertical bar less than space 1`  then the sequence **converges**\n  \n    - `open vertical bar r close vertical bar less than 1`**  **is the same as   $-1<r<1$\n    - In this case the sum to infinity can be calculated\n  - If  `open vertical bar r close vertical bar greater or equal than 1`  then the sequence **does not converge**\n  \n    - `open vertical bar r close vertical bar greater or equal than 1`  is the same as  $r\\leq-1$  or  $r\\geq1$\n    - In this case the sum to infinity cannot be calculated\n- If `vertical line r vertical line space less than space 1`, then the sum **converges** to a finite value given by the formula"
  },
  {
   "type": "paragraph",
   "md": "`S subscript infinity equals fraction numerator a over denominator 1 minus r end fraction space comma space blank open vertical bar r close vertical bar less than 1`"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- $a$ is the **first term**\n- $r$ is the **common ratio**\n- The formula is given on the exam **formula sheet**\n\n  - So you don't need to remember it\n  - But you do need to know how to use it!"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- Always check the `open vertical bar r close vertical bar less than 1` condition before calculating a sum to infinity\n\n  - Marks may depend on showing this in your working"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "The first three terms of a geometric sequence are  $6,2,\\frac{2}{3}$.  Show that the sum to infinity of the series exists, and then find the sum to infinity.\n\n> *To show the sum to infinity exists we need to know the value of the common ratio *$r$\n\n> *We can find this by dividing a term by the term immediately before it *\n\n$r=\\frac{2}{6}=\\frac{1}{3}$\n\n> *This satisfies *`open vertical bar r close vertical bar less than 1`*, so the series converges and the sum to infinity exists*\n\n`stretchy vertical line r stretchy vertical line bold less than bold 1`*, ***so the series converges**\n\n> *Now use the sum to infinity formula  *$S_{\\infty}=\\frac{a}{1-r}$\n\n> *Here  *$a=6$*  and  *$r=\\frac{1}{3}$\n\n`table row cell S subscript infinity end cell equals cell fraction numerator 6 over denominator 1 minus 1 third end fraction end cell row blank equals cell fraction numerator 6 over denominator open parentheses 2 over 3 close parentheses end fraction end cell row blank equals cell 6 cross times 3 over 2 end cell end table`\n\n$S_{\\infty}=9$"
  }
 ]
}